<p>An alliance of al-Qaida-linked Jama’at Nusrat al-Islam wal-Muslimin and the Tuareg-led Azawad Liberation Front launched coordinated attacks on April 25 against military and government targets across Mali, including Bamako, Kati, Gao, Sévaré and Kidal. Mali’s government said Defence Minister Gen. Sadio Camara was killed during an attack on his residence in Kati. The FLA said it took control of Kidal, a strategic northern city, after Malian troops and Russian fighters withdrew; Russian fighters said they had pulled out after separatist attacks. Reports citing local and allied sources said the withdrawal allowed jihadist and Tuareg rebel forces to seize the stronghold. Prime Minister Abdoulaye Maiga said the attacks sought to seize power, destabilize state institutions and derail Mali’s transition process. Russia’s Defence Ministry described the assault as an attempted coup by JNIM and the FLA. Russian officials later urged stability and warned that rebels were regrouping.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/5RoUpJfvCxTZQY1n4k6DRCKerAGAT66udd4cd64HdgS5l1Bg_fC6kiHg0ZOdFvDA5_vYkcCVPJ8ZsWFBMigDGeJDDc8zqmlTpJfthpq7CBZKDcJxJXK4hCV2LfKppduZ_iOckeh0q3Z_QQyrq356pq38SAfW8duPeRfUrkmQR4g2jr7yt8KkdRW6PNutBM4lfTFlwxvZHC2FTesntsE3CqUWnLDRG6RCzL2Ws4xSz_J93vzp1Lyn3SV7esFdWUOan3Yql98' alt='A Malian soldier takes position during an attack on the main military base in Kati, near Bamako, on April 25, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Kati significance: The heaviest clashes were reported in Kati, about 15 kilometres from Bamako and a stronghold of the military authorities who took power in 2021.</li><li>Rebel alliance: JNIM and the FLA have different agendas, with JNIM affiliated to al-Qaida and the FLA rooted in the Tuareg separatist movement in northern Mali.</li><li>Russian withdrawal: Le Monde reported that at least 400 Africa Corps paramilitaries were evacuated from Kidal under armed escort after FLA fighters entered the city.</li><li>Leadership questions: Africanews reported that junta leader Gen. Assimi Goïta had not appeared publicly after the attacks.</li><li>Additional gains: JNIM claimed Mali’s army had left Tessit as the insurgency spread north, while government and army spokespeople did not immediately respond to requests for comment.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mali’s prime minister: Abdoulaye Maiga said unnamed sponsors enabled the violence and accused attackers of trying to sow fear, break national cohesion and weaken resolve. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/africa/639209-terrorists-attempted-mali-military-government-overthrow/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Russian Africa Corps: The Russian-backed force said it was continuing operations in other parts of Mali after the Kidal pullout, without giving locations or a timetable. (<a href='https://eastleighvoice.co.ke/africa/339860/russian-backed-military-unit-withdraws-from-malis-kidal-after-coordinated-attacks'>Eastleigh Voice</a>)</li><li>Senior Malian official: A senior Malian official told RFI that Russian forces had “betrayed” Bamako by withdrawing from Kidal before rebel forces entered the city. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604280333.html'>AllAfrica/RFI</a>)</li><li>Political analyst Josiasse Assemon: Assemon said the attacks combined covert infiltration, strikes on major cities and cooperation between rival armed groups in an effort to destabilize the state. (<a href='http://www.africanews.com/2026/04/27/mali-under-pressure-as-coordinated-attacks-aim-to-destabilise-state-interview/'>Africanews</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/28/taking-power-in-mali-might-be-a-stretch-but-insurgents-can-force-hand-of-weakened-regime'>Taking power in Mali might be a stretch but insurgents can force hand of weakened regime</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/largest-coordinated-attack-decade-militants-rebels-mali-132432507'>What to know about the largest coordinated attack i